---
// The toolkit test bench, shown only under CACHING_DRAFT_PREVIEW=1 at
// /learn/databases/caching/toolkit/. It exercises every part of the shared
// toolkit inside the real article layout. It is not an article.
import Term from '../Term.astro';
import Legend from '../Legend.astro';
import CacheAsideDemo from './CacheAsideDemo.astro';
import LoadChartDemo from './LoadChartDemo.astro';
import LatencyDemo from './LatencyDemo.astro';
import RaceDemo from './RaceDemo.astro';
import OutageChartDemo from './OutageChartDemo.astro';
import LabelsDemo from './LabelsDemo.astro';
import TimelineDemo from './TimelineDemo.astro';
import GridDemo from './GridDemo.astro';
import LaneLabelDemo from './LaneLabelDemo.astro';
---
<p>This page tests the shared figure toolkit for the application caching collection. It is a bench for checking components in both themes and at phone width, not an article. Each section uses one part of the toolkit; the source is in <code>src/components/caching/shared/demo/</code>.</p>
<p>Prose and figures share one colour vocabulary. A <Term kind="client" mark>request</Term> leaves a client and reaches the <Term kind="app">application</Term>, which asks <Term kind="redis">Redis</Term> first. A <Term kind="hit" mark>hit</Term> returns from the cache; a <Term kind="miss" mark>miss</Term> continues to the <Term kind="database">database</Term>, and the application <Term kind="write" mark>writes</Term> the value back. A cached copy older than the database is a <Term kind="stale" mark>stale value</Term>, a key reaching its time to live is an <Term kind="expiry" mark>expiry</Term>, and a request that fails is an <Term kind="error" mark>error</Term>.</p>

<h2>A live simulation</h2>
<p>Figure 1 uses <code>createScene</code> for the drawing, <code>mountSim</code> for the controls, and a pure model built from <code>Simulation</code>, <code>poissonArrivals</code>, <code>zipf</code> and <code>LruCache</code>. With room for a fifth of the keys, watch how many requests still reach the database, then drag the slider.</p>
<CacheAsideDemo number={1} />

<h2>A live chart</h2>
<p>Figure 2 runs the same model without animation at 200 requests a second and plots database reads with <code>lineChart</code>. The cache starts empty, so the first seconds show the cost of a cold cache.</p>
<LoadChartDemo number={2} />

<h2>A distribution and a comparison</h2>
<p>Figure 3 has no clock. One slider redraws a <code>histogram</code> with percentile markers and a <code>barChart</code> of means.</p>
<LatencyDemo number={3} />

<h2>A stepped race</h2>
<p>Figure 4 uses <code>sequenceFigure</code>. Step through it with Back and Next, or press Play to advance at reading pace. The lane values under each heading change as the steps run.</p>
<RaceDemo number={4} />

<h2>Bands, event markers and a log axis</h2>
<p>Figure 5 uses <code>lineChart</code> with a shaded band, event markers, ticks every 60 seconds (<code>tickStep</code>) and, in the lower chart, a logarithmic y axis. The slider moves between uneven <code>stops</code> and shows custom <code>labels</code>.</p>
<OutageChartDemo number={5} />

<h2>Labels that do not collide</h2>
<p>Figure 6 places four close percentile markers on a <code>histogram</code>; their labels take as many rows as they need. The choice switches the <code>barChart</code> labels between above, beside and inside the bars.</p>
<LabelsDemo number={6} />

<h2>A thread timeline in microseconds</h2>
<p>Figure 7 uses <code>threadTimeline</code> for a model whose clock counts microseconds: <code>timeUnit="us"</code> on the figure labels the clock and the Step button, <code>timeScale</code> slows it for the screen, and <code>poissonArrivals</code> takes <code>unitsPerSecond</code>. Two <code>ActionButton</code>s sit in the controls bar: one runs a named action, the other skips ahead.</p>
<TimelineDemo number={7} />

<h2>A grid of cells</h2>
<p>Figure 8 uses <code>cellGrid</code> twice: wide cells with key names and row labels, then small cells for memory pages.</p>
<GridDemo number={8} />

<h2>Long lane names</h2>
<p>Figure 9 has five lanes. At phone width the first lane uses its <code>short</code> label, and the others wrap or shrink to fit their boxes.</p>
<LaneLabelDemo number={9} />

<h2>The legend on its own</h2>
<p>Every kind pairs a colour with a shape, so the figures can be read without relying on hue.</p>
<Legend items={['client', 'hit', 'miss', 'stale', 'write', 'error', 'expiry']} />

